        riskRating: 7,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/C190/c190.glb", model3DScale: 0.09, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.16, y: 0, z: 0.44 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights", "headlights2"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls"] }, author: "Mercedes-Benz AMG GT 2016 (https://skfb.ly/pqDuN) by 3D Cars Studio is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 50, meshGlowTaillight: 14, meshGlowDrl: 28, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The Facelift 1 AMG GT introduces the aggressive Panamericana grille and a slight 14 hp power bump, sharpening its visceral, front-mid engine character. Because the heavy engine sits entirely behind the front axle and the transmission sits at the rear, the balance is superb (47:53). You practically sit on the rear axle. The M178 V8 barks and cracks aggressively even at low speeds, and the dry-sump system allows it to sit incredibly low in the chassis. The 476 hp power delivery is raw and instantaneous, and the ultra-stiff chassis communicates every texture of the road directly to the steering wheel.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From an engineering perspective, the M178 in the AMG GT is mechanically brilliant but requires a completely different mindset for maintenance. The dry-sump oil system is the biggest trap for new mechanics or DIY owners. You cannot just use a dipstick while the engine is cold. The car must be at exactly 100°C oil temperature, and checked within seconds of shutting off (or while idling, depending on exact model year spec). Overfilling the tank is the #1 cause of blown seals in this car.\n\nSecondly, the transaxle layout means the engine is pushed so far back into the firewall that any major work on the rear of the engine (like the timing chain) requires massive labor. The torque tube connecting the engine to the rear DCT transmission contains a carbon-fiber driveshaft and bearings. If you hear a high-pitched whine from the center console, those bearings are failing, which is a brutally expensive repair. \n\nFinally, the 'Hot-V' turbo layout bakes the plastic coolant lines until they shatter. Proactively upgrade them to billet aluminum. Also, be exceptionally careful with the $1,500 Lithium-Ion starter battery; if you leave the car parked without a specific lithium trickle charger, it will fully discharge and permanently 'brick' its internal management system. Maintain it meticulously, and it is a bomb-proof track weapon.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$3,000 - $6,000+ / year (The AMG GT carries 'Supercar Tax' on parts and labor. A Lithium-Ion battery replacement alone is $1,500+. The transaxle DCT fluid change is $450+. You MUST budget for the inevitable Hot-V Turbo Coolant Line replacement ($600 for billet upgrades and labor) and Intake Valve Walnut Blasting ($600) every 80k km. If the Torque Tube bearings fail, expect a $4,000+ repair bill. Strict 8,000 km oil changes ($180) are mandatory, and checking the dry-sump level incorrectly can ruin the engine.)",
