        sisterModels: [ { name: "Mercedes-AMG GT S / GT C / GT R (US Spec)", note: "Higher output versions of the exact same M178 engine, tuned for the North American market without OPF restrictions." } ], 
        tuningPotential: [
            { stage: "Stage 1 (ECU Remap)", power: "550 - 580 hp", notes: "MASSIVE GAINS. Because the US-Spec AMG GT lacks the OPF, tuning is incredibly rewarding. A simple OBD flash easily unlocks 550+ hp without the severe EGT (Exhaust Gas Temperature) spikes seen in European models. However, tuners MUST implement a strict torque limit map in the lower gears to protect the stock 7-speed transaxle from slipping.", zeroToHundred: "3.5s", hundredToTwoHundred: "8.0s" },
            { stage: "Stage 2 (Downpipes & Tune)", power: "610 - 640 hp", notes: "Requires catless or high-flow downpipes. Produces an incredibly loud, raw V8 exhaust note. To safely handle this power, TCU (Transmission Control Unit) tuning is absolutely mandatory to increase the clamping pressure of the DCT clutch packs.", zeroToHundred: "3.3s", hundredToTwoHundred: "7.4s" }
        ],
        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 476, nm: 630 }, stage1: { hp: 570, nm: 800 }, stage2: { hp: 630, nm: 860 }, stage3: { hp: 710, nm: 950 } },
        riskRating: 7,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/C190/c190.glb", model3DScale: 0.09, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.16, y: 0, z: 0.44 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights", "headlights2"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls"] }, author: "Mercedes-Benz AMG GT 2016 (https://skfb.ly/pqDuN) by 3D Cars Studio is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 50, meshGlowTaillight: 14, meshGlowDrl: 28, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The US-Spec AMG GT is a visceral, front-mid engine brute that benefits massively from lacking the European OPF exhaust restrictions. The M178 V8 barks, cracks, and roars aggressively even at low speeds, sounding much more like the original 2015 models. Because the heavy engine sits entirely behind the front axle and the transmission sits at the rear, the balance is superb (47:53). The 476 hp power delivery is raw and instantaneous, and the ultra-stiff chassis communicates every texture of the road directly to the steering wheel.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From a mechanic's perspective, the US-spec M178 is mechanically brilliant and spares you the nightmare of OPF thermal management. However, it replaces that issue with hyper-sensitive EPA emissions systems. The EVAP charcoal canister and purge valve fail frequently; if an owner routinely 'tops off' the gas tank after the pump clicks, raw fuel floods the canister, destroying it and throwing a persistent check engine light that prevents passing state inspections.\n\nLike all GTs, the dry-sump oil system is a trap. You cannot just use a dipstick while cold. The car must be at exactly 212°F (100°C) oil temperature. Overfilling the tank blows out engine seals. Secondly, the transaxle layout means accessing the rear-mounted timing chain requires massive labor. The torque tube connecting the engine to the rear DCT contains a carbon-fiber driveshaft and bearings. If you hear a high-pitched whine from the center console, those bearings are failing, which is a brutally expensive repair. \n\nFinally, the 'Hot-V' turbo layout still bakes the plastic coolant lines until they shatter. Proactively upgrade them to billet aluminum. Also, be exceptionally careful with the $1,500 Lithium-Ion starter battery; if you leave the car parked without a specific lithium trickle charger, it will permanently 'brick'.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$3,000 - $6,000+ / year (The AMG GT carries 'Supercar Tax' on parts and labor. A Lithium-Ion battery replacement alone is $1,500+. The transaxle DCT fluid change is $500+. You MUST budget for the inevitable Hot-V Turbo Coolant Line replacement ($700 for billet upgrades) and Intake Valve Walnut Blasting ($650) every 50k miles. If the EVAP system floods or the Torque Tube bearings collapse, expect a $4,000+ repair bill. Strict 5,000-mile oil changes ($200) are mandatory, and checking the dry-sump level incorrectly can ruin the engine.)",
