        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 510, nm: 650 }, stage1: { hp: 590, nm: 790 }, stage2: { hp: 640, nm: 850 }, stage3: { hp: 700, nm: 950 } },
        riskRating: 7,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/C190/c190.glb", model3DScale: 0.09, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.16, y: 0, z: 0.44 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights", "headlights2"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls"] }, author: "Mercedes-Benz AMG GT 2016 (https://skfb.ly/pqDuN) by 3D Cars Studio is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 50, meshGlowTaillight: 14, meshGlowDrl: 28, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The GT S is where the C190 chassis truly comes alive. With 510 hp and 650 Nm, it possesses the mid-range sledgehammer punch that the base GT slightly lacks. The crucial addition is the electronic limited-slip differential, which perfectly manages the 47:53 rear-biased weight distribution, allowing you to deploy power out of corners with astonishing aggression. Switching to 'RACE' mode sharpens the DCT shifts into violent, immediate gear changes, while the active exhaust system provides a thunderous, crackling V8 soundtrack that defines the AMG brand.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From a mechanic's perspective, the GT S shares the exact same brilliance and pitfalls as the base GT, with the added complexity of the electronic differential. This E-Diff is fantastic on track but generates immense heat; its fluid must be changed religiously (every 30k km, or 15k for track cars) to prevent actuator failure.\n\nThe dry-sump oil system remains the biggest trap. You cannot just use a dipstick while cold. The car must be at exactly 100°C oil temperature. Overfilling the tank blows out engine seals. Secondly, the transaxle layout means accessing the rear-mounted timing chain requires massive labor. The torque tube connecting the engine to the rear DCT contains a carbon-fiber driveshaft and bearings. If you hear a high-pitched whine from the center console, those bearings are failing, which is a brutally expensive repair. \n\nFinally, the 'Hot-V' turbo layout still bakes the plastic coolant lines until they shatter. Proactively upgrade them to billet aluminum. Also, be exceptionally careful with the $1,500 Lithium-Ion starter battery; if you leave the car parked without a specific lithium trickle charger, it will fully discharge and permanently 'brick'.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$3,000 - $6,000+ / year (The AMG GT carries 'Supercar Tax' on parts and labor. A Lithium-Ion battery replacement alone is $1,500+. The transaxle DCT fluid change is $450+. You MUST budget for the inevitable Hot-V Turbo Coolant Line replacement ($600 for billet upgrades) and Intake Valve Walnut Blasting ($600) every 80k km. If the Torque Tube bearings fail, expect a $4,000+ repair bill. Strict 8,000 km oil changes ($180) are mandatory, and checking the dry-sump level incorrectly can ruin the engine.)",
