        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/C190/c190.glb", model3DScale: 0.09, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.16, y: 0, z: 0.44 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights", "headlights2"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls"] }, author: "Mercedes-Benz AMG GT 2016 (https://skfb.ly/pqDuN) by 3D Cars Studio is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 50, meshGlowTaillight: 14, meshGlowDrl: 28, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The AMG GT R, aptly named the 'Beast of the Green Hell', is a violent, track-focused weapon. It boasts a 57mm wider rear track, massive 325-section rear tires, and an aggressive aerodynamics package featuring an active carbon-fiber underbody profile that sucks the car to the road at speed. The defining feature is the yellow 9-stage AMG Traction Control dial in the center console, allowing the driver to perfectly dial in slip angles on track. The larger turbos provide a savage 585 hp top-end rush, while the Active Rear Steering makes the long-nosed chassis pivot with terrifying agility.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From an engineering standpoint, the GT R is a monumental leap over the standard GT. However, from a mechanic's perspective, it introduces a layer of hypercar-level complexity. The engine block is the familiar M178, but the turbochargers are significantly larger and push 1.35 bar. This generates an inferno inside the 'Hot-V'. The factory plastic coolant lines *will* bake, shatter, and dump coolant. Proactively upgrading these to billet aluminum is not optional for a track-driven GT R; it is mandatory for survival.\n\nThe chassis introduces equal complexity. The GT R features a carbon-fiber torque tube to save weight, but the internal driveshaft bearings still wear out under the immense 700 Nm load. If you hear a high-pitched whine from the center tunnel, a brutally expensive replacement is imminent. The Active Rear Steering (ARS) uses electromechanical actuators exposed to extreme heat and track debris; if they fail, the car goes into severe limp mode.\n\nFinally, the electronics are highly integrated. The brilliant 9-stage traction control relies entirely on perfect data from the wheel speed sensors and the electronic limited-slip differential. If the E-Diff fluid is neglected and the actuator overheats, the entire traction control system shuts down. Check the dry sump oil strictly at 100°C, keep the $1,500 lithium battery on a trickle charger, and respect the astronomical replacement cost of the Carbon Ceramic brakes if they oxidize from track heat without proper cool-down laps.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$5,000 - $10,000+ / year (The GT R carries immense 'Supercar Tax', exacerbated by extreme track wear. A set of Michelin Cup 2 tires ($1,800) will vanish in a few track weekends. Carbon Ceramic rotor replacement is $15,000+ if oxidized. A Lithium-Ion battery replacement is $1,500+. You MUST budget for the inevitable Hot-V Turbo Coolant Line replacement ($600 for billet upgrades) and Intake Valve Walnut Blasting ($600) every 80k km. If the ARS actuators or Torque Tube bearings fail, expect a $4,000+ repair bill.)",
