        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/C190/c190.glb", model3DScale: 0.09, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.16, y: 0, z: 0.44 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights", "headlights2"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls"] }, author: "Mercedes-Benz AMG GT 2016 (https://skfb.ly/pqDuN) by 3D Cars Studio is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 50, meshGlowTaillight: 14, meshGlowDrl: 28, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The AMG GT R Pro is essentially a street-legal GT3/GT4 race car. The engine remains the familiar 585 hp unit, but the chassis is where the magic (and brutality) lies. Standard rubber suspension bushings are replaced by solid uniball spherical bearings, translating every pebble on the road directly into the chassis. The manually adjustable coilovers allow for perfect track corner-weighting. The massive aerodynamic package (dive planes, front splitter, Gurney flap) creates immense downforce, sucking the car to the tarmac. It is vicious, loud, stiff, and utterly dominant on a circuit.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The GT R Pro is where mechanics pull their hair out, not because of the engine, but because of the chassis. The engine is the identical 585 hp M178 found in the standard GT R, complete with the same 'Hot-V' overheating issues that necessitate billet coolant lines. However, the Pro's chassis is a completely different animal. \n\nBy replacing traditional rubber suspension bushings with solid uniball spherical bearings, Mercedes created a car that steers with telepathic precision. But on the street, these uniballs suffer. Dust, dirt, and vibrations wear them out incredibly fast, leading to severe knocking and clunking sounds from the suspension. The manually adjustable coilovers require specialized spanner wrenches and a deep understanding of corner-weighting; if you blindly twist the collars, you will completely ruin the car's handling balance. \n\nFurthermore, the Pro comes standard with Carbon Ceramic Brakes (CCB). On track, if you do not perform strict cool-down laps, the extreme heat will literally oxidize the carbon fibers inside the rotor. Once they oxidize and lose weight (checked via special weighing tools, not thickness), you are looking at a $15,000+ replacement bill. The massive front carbon-fiber splitter is also incredibly low; shattering it on a curb is a frequent and devastatingly expensive mistake. This is a race car with license plates; maintain it exactly like one.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$8,000 - $15,000+ / year (The GT R Pro carries astronomical running costs if tracked. A set of Michelin Cup 2 tires will vanish in a few track weekends. If you oxidize the Carbon Ceramic rotors, add $15,000+. Uniball suspension joints wear out and require expensive replacements to cure knocking. You MUST budget for the inevitable Hot-V Turbo Coolant Line replacement ($600 for billet upgrades) and Intake Valve Walnut Blasting. Smashing the carbon front splitter is a $4,000+ mistake.)",
