        sisterModels: [ { name: "Mercedes-AMG GT 53 (X290)", note: "The exact same vehicle and engine mechanically, but the 53 features a factory tune unlocking 435 hp and standard 4MATIC+ AWD." }, { name: "Mercedes-Benz CLS 53 / E 53 AMG", note: "Shares the identical M256 inline-6 mild-hybrid powertrain in a slightly different chassis." } ], 
        tuningPotential: [
            { stage: "Stage 1 (ECU Remap)", power: "420 - 440 hp", notes: "EXCELLENT VALUE. Because the GT 43 is essentially a software-detuned GT 53, a simple OBD Stage 1 flash instantly brings the power output up to, or slightly past, the factory GT 53 levels. The massive low-end torque from the electric compressor (eZV) combined with higher turbo boost makes it brutally fast off the line. However, tuners must carefully map around the sensitive 48V hybrid torque limits to avoid limp mode.", zeroToHundred: "4.4s", hundredToTwoHundred: "10.5s" },
            { stage: "Stage 2 (Downpipes & Tune)", power: "460 - 480 hp", notes: "Requires high-flow downpipes. The inline-6 produces a fantastic, high-pitched scream. To safely handle this power, TCU (Transmission Control Unit) tuning is recommended. Extreme tuning on the M256 is difficult because overriding the Bosch MG1 hybrid integration requires bypassing complex torque-monitoring algorithms.", zeroToHundred: "4.1s", hundredToTwoHundred: "9.8s" }
        ],
        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 367, nm: 500 }, stage1: { hp: 430, nm: 580 }, stage2: { hp: 470, nm: 650 }, stage3: { hp: 520, nm: 720 } },
        riskRating: 7,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/X290/x290.glb", model3DScale: 0.1, model3DRotation: [0,0,0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.17, y: -0.01, z: 0.5 } },  customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlightsfix2.001", "headlightsfix.001"], taillights: ["taillightsfix.003"], drllight: ["drls.003"] }, author: "Mercedes AMG GT 63 (https://skfb.ly/o8Zpu) by Saimon93 is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",  lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 20, meshGlowTaillight: 14, meshGlowDrl: 60, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The AMG GT 43 4-Door is a masterpiece of technological smoothness. The M256 inline-6 is inherently perfectly balanced, and the complete lack of front belt-driven accessories makes the engine whisper-quiet at idle. When you press the throttle, the 48V electric compressor (eZV) spools in 0.3 seconds, providing 250 Nm of immediate electric torque fill. This completely masks any traditional turbo lag. It surges forward with the seamless, relentless wave of an electric car, but crescendos into a raspy, metallic inline-6 howl as the main turbocharger takes over. In this RWD configuration, it feels remarkably agile for a 2-ton luxury coupe.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From an engineering standpoint, the M256 is a revolutionary 'beltless' design. From an independent mechanic's perspective, it is a brilliant engine wrapped in a terrifyingly expensive electrical architecture. \n\nMechanically, the ICE core is practically immortal. However, eliminating the $100 serpentine belt means the A/C compressor is now a $1,400 48V electric unit, and the water pump is a $700 48V electric unit. If any of these 48V components short out, or if the 48V lithium-ion battery deeply discharges, the entire vehicle is 'bricked'—it will not start, and it must be towed. The 48V Battery Management System (BMS) software is notoriously temperamental; ensure it has the latest dealer updates.\n\nFurthermore, because the Integrated Starter Generator (ISG) sits between the engine and the transmission, the timing chain was relocated to the rear firewall. If you stretch oil intervals and that chain wears out, replacing it requires dropping the transmission—an astronomically expensive labor bill. Keep the oil fresh, keep the battery on a trickle charger, and enjoy the futuristic powertrain.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$2,000 - $4,500 / year (The GT 43 carries extreme financial risk not from mechanical failure, but from 48V electrical failures. If the 48V lithium battery fails out of warranty, repairs exceed $2,500. The 48V Electric A/C Compressor is $1,400+. Traditional maintenance is also premium: Intake Manifold Walnut Blasting (500 EUR) is needed every 100k km. Strict 10,000 km oil changes (150 EUR) are absolutely non-negotiable to protect the incredibly inaccessible rear timing chain.)",
