        sisterModels: [ { name: "Mercedes-AMG GT 53 4MATIC+ (X290)", note: "The exact same vehicle mechanically, but features a factory tune unlocking 435 hp." }, { name: "Mercedes-Benz CLS 53 / E 53 AMG", note: "Shares the identical M256 inline-6 mild-hybrid 4MATIC+ powertrain." } ], 
        tuningPotential: [
            { stage: "Stage 1 (ECU Remap)", power: "430 - 440 hp", notes: "EXCELLENT VALUE. The GT 43 is a software-detuned GT 53. An OBD Stage 1 flash instantly brings the power output up to, or slightly past, the factory GT 53 levels. The massive low-end torque from the electric compressor (eZV) combined with the grip of the 4MATIC+ system makes it brutally fast off the line. Tuners must carefully map around the sensitive 48V hybrid torque limits.", zeroToHundred: "4.2s", hundredToTwoHundred: "10.4s" },
            { stage: "Stage 2 (Downpipes & Tune)", power: "470 - 490 hp", notes: "Requires high-flow downpipes. The inline-6 produces a fantastic, high-pitched scream. To safely handle this power, TCU (Transmission Control Unit) tuning is highly recommended to manage the 4MATIC+ clutch pressures. Extreme tuning on the M256 requires bypassing complex Bosch MG1 hybrid torque-monitoring algorithms.", zeroToHundred: "3.9s", hundredToTwoHundred: "9.5s" },
            { stage: "Stage 3 (Upgraded Turbo & Fueling)", power: "530 - 550 hp", notes: "Involves upgrading the primary BorgWarner exhaust gas turbocharger and installing a higher-capacity High Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP). The 48V eZV electric compressor remains stock. At this power level, the 4MATIC+ transfer case clutches become the critical point of failure and require rigorous maintenance or hardware upgrades.", zeroToHundred: "3.5s", hundredToTwoHundred: "8.6s" }
        ],
        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 367, nm: 500 }, stage1: { hp: 435, nm: 580 }, stage2: { hp: 480, nm: 650 }, stage3: { hp: 540, nm: 720 } },
        riskRating: 7,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/X290/x290.glb", model3DScale: 0.1, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.17, y: -0.01, z: 0.5 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlightsfix2.001", "headlightsfix.001"], taillights: ["taillightsfix.003"], drllight: ["drls.003"] }, author: "Mercedes AMG GT 63 (https://skfb.ly/o8Zpu) by Saimon93 is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 20, meshGlowTaillight: 14, meshGlowDrl: 60, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The AMG GT 43 4MATIC+ 4-Door marries the technological smoothness of the inline-6 with relentless all-weather traction. The M256 engine is whisper-quiet at idle due to the lack of belt-driven accessories. When you press the throttle, the 48V electric compressor (eZV) spools in 0.3 seconds, providing 250 Nm of immediate electric torque fill. Thanks to the 4MATIC+ AWD system, none of this torque is wasted; the car surges off the line with zero slip and zero turbo lag, eventually crescendoing into a raspy, metallic inline-6 howl as the main turbocharger takes over. It feels lighter and far more agile than its 2.1-ton curb weight suggests.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From an engineering standpoint, the M256 is a revolutionary 'beltless' design. From an independent mechanic's perspective, it is a brilliant ICE core wrapped in a terrifyingly expensive electrical and drivetrain architecture. \n\nMechanically, the engine block is practically immortal. However, eliminating the $100 serpentine belt means the A/C compressor is now a $1,400 48V electric unit, and the water pump is a $700 48V electric unit. If any of these components short out, or if the 48V lithium-ion battery deeply discharges, the entire vehicle is 'bricked'—it will not start, and it must be towed. The 48V Battery Management System (BMS) software is notoriously temperamental; ensure it has the latest dealer updates.\n\nFurthermore, because the Integrated Starter Generator (ISG) sits between the engine and the transmission, the timing chain was relocated to the rear firewall. If you stretch oil intervals and that chain wears out, replacing it requires dropping the transmission—an astronomically expensive labor bill. Finally, the addition of the 4MATIC+ transfer case adds another layer of maintenance. The fluid inside the transfer case must be changed every 50,000 km, or the clutches will bind and cause shuddering during tight turns. Keep the fluids fresh, keep the battery on a trickle charger, and respect the complexity of the hybrid-AWD system.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$2,500 - $5,000 / year (The GT 43 4MATIC+ carries extreme financial risk from 48V electrical failures and AWD wear. If the 48V lithium battery fails out of warranty, repairs exceed $2,500. The 48V Electric A/C Compressor is $1,400+. Traditional maintenance is also premium: Intake Manifold Walnut Blasting (500 EUR) is needed every 100k km, and Transfer Case fluid changes (120 EUR) are mandatory every 50k km. Strict 10,000 km oil changes (150 EUR) are absolutely non-negotiable to protect the incredibly inaccessible rear timing chain.)",
