        sisterModels: [ { name: "Mercedes-AMG GT 43 (US Spec)", note: "The US market 43 uses the exact same M256 setup, but the 53 unlocks 435 hp directly from the factory." } ], 
        tuningPotential: [
            { stage: "Stage 1 (ECU Remap)", power: "480 - 500 hp", notes: "VERY STRONG GAINS. Because the GT 53 is the 'unlocked' version of the M256 from the factory, a Stage 1 flash pushes the inline-6 to nearly 500 hp safely. The US spec is particularly receptive because it does not suffer from OPF exhaust restrictions. Tuners must carefully map around the sensitive 48V hybrid torque limits and ensure a 93 AKI specific tune.", zeroToHundred: "3.9s", hundredToTwoHundred: "9.7s" },
            { stage: "Stage 2 (Downpipes & Tune)", power: "530 - 550 hp", notes: "Requires high-flow downpipes. The lack of OPF on US models means Stage 2 is slightly less complicated physically (no OPF sensors to trick), but still requires TCU tuning to manage the 4MATIC+ clutch pressures. Bypassing the Bosch MG1 hybrid integration is highly complex.", zeroToHundred: "3.6s", hundredToTwoHundred: "8.9s" },
            { stage: "Stage 3 (Upgraded Turbo & Fueling)", power: "600 - 620 hp", notes: "Involves upgrading the primary BorgWarner exhaust gas turbocharger and installing a higher-capacity HPFP. The 48V eZV electric compressor remains stock but continues to provide low-end fill. At this power level, the 4MATIC+ transfer case clutches and 9-speed TCT clutch packs become the critical points of failure and require hardware upgrades.", zeroToHundred: "3.3s", hundredToTwoHundred: "8.1s" }
        ],
        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 435, nm: 520 }, stage1: { hp: 490, nm: 610 }, stage2: { hp: 540, nm: 680 }, stage3: { hp: 610, nm: 750 } },
        riskRating: 7,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/X290/x290.glb", model3DScale: 0.1, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.17, y: -0.01, z: 0.5 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlightsfix2.001", "headlightsfix.001"], taillights: ["taillightsfix.003"], drllight: ["drls.003"] }, author: "Mercedes AMG GT 63 (https://skfb.ly/o8Zpu) by Saimon93 is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 20, meshGlowTaillight: 14, meshGlowDrl: 60, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The AMG GT 53 US-Spec is a brilliant execution of modern hybrid performance. Devoid of the restrictive Otto Particulate Filters (OPF) mandated in Europe, the American version breathes better and sounds noticeably louder and raspier out of the box. The M256 engine is whisper-quiet at idle due to the lack of belt-driven accessories, but when provoked, the 48V electric compressor (eZV) spools in 0.3 seconds. This provides immediate electric torque fill, catapulting the 4,600 lb car off the line with the ruthless efficiency of the 4MATIC+ AWD system. It feels impossibly smooth yet aggressively capable.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From a mechanic's perspective, the US-spec M256 provides a minor sigh of relief: you don't have to deal with melting OPF (Otto Particulate Filter) cores or the associated sensor limp modes found on Euro models. However, that is where the good news ends. The foundation is still a terrifyingly expensive 'beltless' electrical architecture. Eliminating the $100 serpentine belt means the A/C compressor is now a $1,500 48V electric unit, and the water pump is a $750 48V electric unit. If any of these components short out, or if the $2,500 48V lithium-ion battery deeply discharges, the entire vehicle is 'bricked'—it will not start, and it must be towed.\n\nMechanically, the engine block is practically immortal. However, because the ISG is sandwiched between the engine and transmission, the timing chain was relocated to the rear firewall. The standard US factory oil change interval is 10,000 miles. DO NOT follow this. If you stretch oil intervals and that rear chain wears out, replacing it requires dropping the transmission—an astronomically expensive labor bill. The extra torque of the 53 tune places higher stress on the 4MATIC+ transfer case. Change its fluid every 30,000 miles, keep the battery on a trickle charger, and respect the complexity of the hybrid-AWD system.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$2,500 - $5,500 / year (The US-spec GT 53 carries extreme financial risk from 48V electrical failures and AWD wear. If the 48V lithium battery fails out of warranty, repairs exceed $2,500. The 48V Electric A/C Compressor is $1,500+. Traditional maintenance is also premium: Intake Manifold Walnut Blasting ($600) is needed every 60k miles, and Transfer Case fluid changes ($120) are mandatory every 30k miles. Strict 5,000-mile oil changes ($150) are absolutely non-negotiable to protect the incredibly inaccessible rear timing chain.)",
