        sisterModels: [ { name: "Mercedes-AMG E 63 S (W213 US Spec)", note: "Shares the identical M177 powertrain, MCT transmission, and 4MATIC+ AWD system." } ], 
        tuningPotential: [
            { stage: "Stage 1 (ECU Remap)", power: "730 - 760 hp", notes: "EXCELLENT GAINS. An OBD Stage 1 flash unlocks the massive potential of the twin-scroll turbos. Because the US-Spec lacks the restrictive OPF, it accepts higher boost curves with lower EGTs than the Euro model. TCU (Transmission Control Unit) tuning is highly recommended to increase the MCT clamping pressure.", zeroToHundred: "2.8s", hundredToTwoHundred: "6.7s" },
            { stage: "Stage 2 (Downpipes & Tune)", power: "790 - 830 hp", notes: "Requires catless or high-flow downpipes. The lack of OPF on US models makes Stage 2 slightly less complicated physically, unlocking a thunderous V8 rumble. TCU tuning is absolutely mandatory at this stage to prevent the wet start-off clutch from slipping under the 1,100+ Nm of torque.", zeroToHundred: "2.6s", hundredToTwoHundred: "6.2s" },
            { stage: "Stage 3 (Upgraded Turbos & HPFP)", power: "920 - 1000+ hp", notes: "Involves removing the factory turbos from the Hot-V and installing massive upgraded units alongside upgraded High Pressure Fuel Pumps (HPFP). The forged block handles this effortlessly. At this level, the AMG SPEEDSHIFT MCT absolutely requires upgraded clutch packs, and the 4MATIC+ transfer case is under extreme stress.", zeroToHundred: "2.4s", hundredToTwoHundred: "5.4s" }
        ],
        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 639, nm: 900 }, stage1: { hp: 745, nm: 1020 }, stage2: { hp: 810, nm: 1100 }, stage3: { hp: 950, nm: 1200 } },
        riskRating: 8,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/X290/x290.glb", model3DScale: 0.1, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.17, y: -0.01, z: 0.5 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlightsfix2.001", "headlightsfix.001"], taillights: ["taillightsfix.003"], drllight: ["drls.003"] }, author: "Mercedes AMG GT 63 (https://skfb.ly/o8Zpu) by Saimon93 is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 20, meshGlowTaillight: 14, meshGlowDrl: 60, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The US-Spec AMG GT 63 S represents the powertrain in its most raw, unbridled form. Devoid of the restrictive European Otto Particulate Filters (OPF) and free from strict EU noise regulations, the M177 breathes deeper and screams louder. It delivers 639 hp and 900 Nm with absolute savagery. The AMG SPEEDSHIFT MCT transmission slams gears, while the 4MATIC+ system provides brutal launch control traction. Engage 'Drift Mode', and the front axle completely decouples, sending all 900 Nm to the rear wheels, turning this massive 4,600 lb luxury coupe into an unhinged, tire-shredding monster. It sounds like artillery fire.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From a mechanic's perspective, the US-spec GT 63 S offers a sigh of relief: you don't have to deal with melting OPF cores or the terrifyingly expensive 48V ISG hybrid system of the inline-6 models. The M177 uses a traditional 12V starter, and the timing chain is at the front of the engine where it belongs.\n\nHowever, you are trading electrical complexity for extreme mechanical brutality. The 'Hot-V' twin-turbos will bake the factory plastic coolant lines until they shatter. Upgrade them to billet aluminum immediately. The immense 900 Nm of torque absolutely shreds the active engine mounts over time. The PCV (oil separator) valves clog frequently; if you ignore a whistling sound, crankcase pressure will build and blow out the Rear Main Seal (RMS), requiring a $3,000+ transmission drop. \n\nDrivetrain maintenance is the difference between a reliable car and a financial disaster. The MCT transmission uses a wet multi-plate clutch; it contaminates the fluid fast. Change the MCT and 4MATIC+ transfer case fluid every 30,000 miles. If the owner uses 'Drift Mode' frequently, that transfer case fluid needs changing even sooner. Watch out for strict US EVAP sensor faults triggering Check Engine Lights, but otherwise, treat the mechanicals with respect and it is a masterpiece.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$3,500 - $7,000 / year (The GT 63 S carries immense 'Supercar Tax' on mechanical components. You MUST budget for the inevitable Hot-V Turbo Coolant Line billet upgrade ($650). If the PCV fails and blows the Rear Main Seal, expect a $3,000+ repair bill. Active Engine Mounts ($1,400) fail regularly under the 900 Nm torque. Consumables are astronomical: a set of Michelin Pilot Sport 4S tires and Brembo 2-piece front rotors ($1,200) will vanish quickly. Strict 5,000-mile oil changes ($160) and frequent MCT/Transfer Case services are non-negotiable.)",
