        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 843, nm: 1400 }, stage1: { hp: 940, nm: 1520 }, stage2: { hp: 1000, nm: 1580 }, stage3: { hp: 1100, nm: 1650 } },
        riskRating: 10,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/X290/x290.glb", model3DScale: 0.1, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.17, y: -0.01, z: 0.5 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlightsfix2.001", "headlightsfix.001"], taillights: ["taillightsfix.003"], drllight: ["drls.003"] }, author: "Mercedes AMG GT 63 (https://skfb.ly/o8Zpu) by Saimon93 is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 20, meshGlowTaillight: 14, meshGlowDrl: 60, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The GT 63 S E Performance completely rewrites the laws of physics. Weighing 2,380 kg (5,250 lbs), it shouldn't be able to move like it does. The P3 hybrid system is not for eco-driving (it has barely 12 km of electric range); it is an F1-derived deployment of power. The 150 kW electric motor on the rear axle eliminates turbo lag entirely. When you initiate launch control, the 1400 Nm of system torque hits with a violence that makes your vision blur. You feel the rear 2-speed electric transmission shift seamlessly into high gear at 140 km/h, ensuring the relentless surge of power never tapers off. It is a terrifyingly capable hyper-sedan.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The GT 63 S E Performance represents the absolute pinnacle of Mercedes-AMG engineering, and simultaneously, a mechanic's greatest nightmare. You are essentially maintaining two high-performance drivetrains bolted together in a 2.4-ton chassis. \n\nLet's start with the ICE. The M177 V8 still suffers from the traditional 'Hot-V' issues. The turbo coolant lines will bake and shatter; upgrade to billet immediately. The PCV system will clog and blow the Rear Main Seal (RMS). However, the real terror lies in the P3 hybrid architecture. The AMG High Performance Battery (HPB) is a 6.1 kWh marvel of F1 technology, utilizing 14 liters of specialized non-conductive dielectric fluid flowing directly over 560 individual cylindrical cells. If this internal cooling circuit springs a leak, the battery destroys itself. A replacement battery is a catastrophic five-figure expense. \n\nThe rear Electric Drive Unit (EDU) houses the motor, the e-LSD, and its own 2-speed transmission. The sheer 1400 Nm torque output literally tears the active engine mounts to shreds over time and cooks the MCT 9G wet clutch if not serviced meticulously. Even the standard Carbon Ceramic Brakes (CCB) are pushed to their absolute limits trying to stop 2.4 tons from hypercar speeds; if they oxidize, it's $15,000 for rotors alone. If this car is out of warranty, you must have an unlimited maintenance budget.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$6,000 - $15,000+ / year (This vehicle carries Hypercar-level financial risk out of warranty. If the F1-derived direct-cooled High-Voltage Battery fails, you are looking at a catastrophic replacement bill. The rear EDU 2-speed transmission is highly complex. Routine maintenance is astronomical: Active Engine Mounts ($1,200) fail constantly under 1400 Nm. A set of Michelin Pilot Sport 4S tires ($1,800) will last roughly 10,000 km under the 2.4-ton weight. If the Carbon Ceramic rotors oxidize from heat, replacement is $15,000+. You MUST proactively fix the V8's Hot-V coolant lines ($600 billet) and service the MCT/EDU fluids relentlessly.)",
