        tuningPotential: [
            { stage: "Stage 1 (ECU Remap)", power: "680 - 710 hp", notes: "INCREDIBLE VALUE. Because the GT 55 is purely a software-detuned GT 63, an OBD Stage 1 flash yields massive gains. You instantly bypass the factory 476 hp limitation, unlocking over 700 hp and 900+ Nm safely. However, tuning an OPF-equipped M177 requires caution. Pushing boost significantly raises EGTs, which will melt the restrictive filters. TCU tuning is mandatory.", zeroToHundred: "3.1s", hundredToTwoHundred: "7.2s" },
            { stage: "Stage 2 (OPF Delete & Downpipes & Tune)", power: "740 - 770 hp", notes: "Requires physical removal of the OPF filters and catless/high-flow downpipes (FOR TRACK USE ONLY). This is absolutely mandatory for Stage 2. It drops EGTs massively, saves the turbos from heat death, and restores the thunderous AMG V8 sound that the factory OPF completely ruined. TCU tuning is absolutely mandatory to protect the MCT.", zeroToHundred: "2.8s", hundredToTwoHundred: "6.4s" },
            { stage: "Stage 3 (Upgraded Turbos & HPFP)", power: "820 - 880+ hp", notes: "Involves removing the factory turbos from the Hot-V and installing larger units alongside upgraded High Pressure Fuel Pumps (HPFP). The OPF must be deleted. At this level, the AMG SPEEDSHIFT MCT requires upgraded clutch packs, and the 4MATIC+ transfer case is under extreme stress.", zeroToHundred: "2.6s", hundredToTwoHundred: "5.8s" }
        ],
        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 476, nm: 700 }, stage1: { hp: 700, nm: 920 }, stage2: { hp: 755, nm: 1000 }, stage3: { hp: 850, nm: 1150 } },
        riskRating: 8,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/C192/c192.glb", model3DScale: 0.1, model3DRotation: [0, 3.14, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.13, y: 0, z: 0.35 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights.001"], taillights: ["taillights.001", "taillights.002"], drllight: ["drls.002"] }, author: ".", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 40, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 15, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The AMG GT 55 (C192) might carry a smaller number on the badge, but beneath the hood lies the exact same 4.0L V8 heart as the mighty GT 63. Mercedes artificially restricted it to 476 hp via software to create a price gap. On the road, it still hits with the deep, baritone M177 roar and uses the incredible grip of the 4MATIC+ system to catapult out of corners. The new C192 chassis is more of a grand tourer than the old GT 2-door, absorbing bumps beautifully while still feeling planted. The Euro 6e OPF filters heavily mute the exhaust note, making it more subtle, but the underlying V8 muscle is undeniably present.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From a mechanic's perspective, the GT 55 is the smartest purchase in the entire modern AMG lineup. You are buying a GT 63 block and turbos at a massive discount. Because the engine is artificially detuned to 476 hp, the internal components are operating well below their mechanical limits, actually increasing the longevity of the engine under factory mapping.\n\nHowever, it is still an M177, which means you inherit its traditional maintenance nightmares. You avoid the terrifyingly expensive 48V EQ Boost electrical architecture found in the 4-cylinder models, but you must aggressively manage the V8's thermal brutality. The 'Hot-V' twin-turbos trap apocalyptic heat, exacerbated by the restrictive Euro 6e OPF filters acting as thermal plugs. The factory plastic coolant lines *will* bake and shatter. Upgrade them to billet aluminum immediately. The PCV (oil separator) valves clog frequently; if you ignore a whistling sound, crankcase pressure will blow out the Rear Main Seal (RMS), requiring a $3,000+ transmission drop. Change the MCT and 4MATIC+ transfer case fluid every 50,000 km, use strictly Low-SAPS oil, and enjoy the incredible tuning potential.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$3,000 - $6,000 / year (The GT 55 carries V8 'Supercar Tax', exacerbated by OPF heat soak. You MUST budget for the inevitable Hot-V Turbo Coolant Line billet upgrade ($600). If the PCV fails and blows the Rear Main Seal, expect a $3,000+ repair bill. Active Engine Mounts ($1,200) fail regularly under the V8's torque and OPF heat. Consumables are massive: a set of Michelin Pilot Sport 4S tires and Brembo 2-piece front rotors ($1,100) will vanish quickly under the AWD chassis. Strict 8,000 km Low-SAPS oil changes ($160) and frequent MCT/Transfer Case services are non-negotiable.)",
