        sisterModels: [ { name: "Mercedes-AMG GT 63 (C192 China 6b)", note: "The GT 55 uses the exact same M177 engine block, transmission, and turbo hardware as the GT 63, but is software-restricted to 476 hp." } ], 
        tuningPotential: [
            { stage: "Stage 1 (ECU Remap)", power: "680 - 710 hp", notes: "INCREDIBLE VALUE BUT HIGH RISK. Because the GT 55 is a software-detuned GT 63, an OBD Stage 1 flash yields massive gains. You instantly bypass the 476 hp limitation, unlocking over 700 hp. However, tuning an China 6b GPF-equipped M177 requires extreme caution. Pushing boost significantly raises EGTs, which will melt the restrictive filters. Tuners must monitor fuel quality and EGTs perfectly.", zeroToHundred: "3.2s", hundredToTwoHundred: "7.3s" },
            { stage: "Stage 2 (GPF Delete & Downpipes & Tune)", power: "740 - 770 hp", notes: "Requires physical removal of the GPF filters and catless downpipes (FOR TRACK USE ONLY). This is absolutely mandatory for Stage 2. It drops EGTs massively, saves the turbos from heat death, and restores the thunderous AMG V8 sound that the factory GPF completely ruined. TCU tuning is absolutely mandatory to protect the MCT.", zeroToHundred: "2.8s", hundredToTwoHundred: "6.4s" },
            { stage: "Stage 3 (Upgraded Turbos & HPFP)", power: "820 - 880+ hp", notes: "Involves removing the factory turbos from the Hot-V and installing larger units alongside upgraded High Pressure Fuel Pumps (HPFP). The GPF must be deleted. At this level, the AMG SPEEDSHIFT MCT requires upgraded clutch packs, and the 4MATIC+ transfer case is under extreme stress.", zeroToHundred: "2.6s", hundredToTwoHundred: "5.8s" }
        ],
        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 476, nm: 700 }, stage1: { hp: 700, nm: 920 }, stage2: { hp: 755, nm: 1000 }, stage3: { hp: 850, nm: 1150 } },
        riskRating: 8,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/C192/c192.glb", model3DScale: 0.1, model3DRotation: [0, 3.14, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.13, y: 0, z: 0.35 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights.001"], taillights: ["taillights.001", "taillights.002"], drllight: ["drls.002"] }, author: ".", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 40, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 15, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The China 6b variant of the AMG GT 55 (C192) offers the same grand touring capability and V8 muscle, but its character is noticeably altered by strict emissions software. In Comfort mode, the throttle response is deliberately lethargic to minimize particulate creation, and the aggressive cold-start catalyst heating routine makes the engine sound coarse initially. Once warm and switched to Sport+, it regains its agility, though the exhaust note is heavily muffled by the massive Gasoline Particulate Filters (GPF). Despite the 476 hp restriction, the 700 Nm of torque combined with the 4MATIC+ system still provides effortless, immediate acceleration.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From a mechanic's perspective, the GT 55 is the smartest purchase in the AMG lineup because you are buying a GT 63 block at a discount. Because the engine is artificially detuned to 476 hp, the internal components are operating well below their mechanical limits.\n\nHowever, the China 6b spec introduces significant long-term headaches. The addition of the Gasoline Particulate Filter (GPF) acts as a massive thermal plug. In heavy Chinese traffic, the car cannot reach the exhaust temperatures required to passively burn off the soot. This leads to premature GPF clogging, triggering sensor faults and severe limp modes. Because it uses large turbos, the heat trapped by the GPF radiates back up into the 'Hot-V'. This bakes the plastic turbo coolant lines and accelerates the failure of the active engine mounts. \n\nFurthermore, the PCV (oil separator) valves clog frequently; if you ignore a whistling sound, crankcase pressure will blow out the Rear Main Seal (RMS), requiring a $3,000+ transmission drop. You must run premium Low-SAPS oil to protect the GPF, use high-quality fuel to prevent detonation from the aggressive China 6b ignition maps, and drive the car hard on the highway to clear the filters.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$3,000 - $6,000 / year (The China 6b GT 55 carries V8 'Supercar Tax', exacerbated by GPF heat soak and urban traffic wear. You MUST budget for the inevitable Hot-V Turbo Coolant Line billet upgrade ($600). GPF replacement or sensor failures are frequent and expensive. If the PCV fails and blows the Rear Main Seal, expect a $3,000+ repair bill. Active Engine Mounts ($1,200) fail regularly under the GPF heat. Strict 5,000 km Low-SAPS oil changes ($160) are absolutely non-negotiable.)",
