        sisterModels: [ { name: "Mercedes-AMG SL 63 (R232)", note: "The GT 63 uses the exact same M177 engine block, transmission, and 4MATIC+ hardware as the SL 63, operating at the same 585 hp output." } ], 
        tuningPotential: [
            { stage: "Stage 1 (ECU Remap)", power: "680 - 710 hp", notes: "MODERATE RISK GAINS. An OBD Stage 1 flash yields massive gains, bypassing the factory 585 hp limitation to reach 700 hp. However, tuning an OPF-equipped GT 63 requires extreme caution. Pushing boost significantly raises Exhaust Gas Temperatures (EGTs), which will melt the restrictive particulate filters. TCU tuning is mandatory.", zeroToHundred: "2.9s", hundredToTwoHundred: "7.0s" },
            { stage: "Stage 2 (OPF Delete & Downpipes & Tune)", power: "740 - 770 hp", notes: "Requires physical removal of the OPF filters and catless/high-flow downpipes (FOR TRACK USE ONLY). This is absolutely mandatory for Stage 2. It drops EGTs massively, saves the turbos from heat death, and restores the thunderous AMG V8 sound that the factory OPF completely ruined. TCU tuning is absolutely mandatory to protect the MCT.", zeroToHundred: "2.7s", hundredToTwoHundred: "6.2s" },
            { stage: "Stage 3 (Upgraded Turbos & HPFP)", power: "820 - 880+ hp", notes: "Involves removing the factory turbos from the Hot-V and installing larger units alongside upgraded High Pressure Fuel Pumps (HPFP). The OPF must be deleted. At this level, the AMG SPEEDSHIFT MCT requires upgraded clutch packs, and the 4MATIC+ transfer case is under extreme stress.", zeroToHundred: "2.5s", hundredToTwoHundred: "5.6s" }
        ],
        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 585, nm: 800 }, stage1: { hp: 700, nm: 920 }, stage2: { hp: 755, nm: 1000 }, stage3: { hp: 850, nm: 1150 } },
        riskRating: 9,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/C192/c192.glb", model3DScale: 0.1, model3DRotation: [0, 3.14, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.13, y: 0, z: 0.35 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights.001"], taillights: ["taillights.001", "taillights.002"], drllight: ["drls.002"] }, author: ".", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 40, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 15, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The GT 63 (C192) OPF edition offers pure, violent V8 muscle, but with an acoustic muzzle. The 585 hp hits with brutal force through the 4MATIC+ system, while the Active Rear-Wheel Steering makes the heavy chassis pivot remarkably well. However, the introduction of the OPF and strict UN-R51.03 acoustic software management in this Euro 6d/6e variant drastically mutes the exhaust note; it lacks the violent, barking V8 thunder of older AMG models. The car is incredibly fast, but many owners instantly install an Exhaust Valve Module or delete the OPF to claw back the lost acoustic drama.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From an engineering perspective, the M177 in the OPF-equipped AMG GT 63 is a mechanically brilliant engine forced to breathe through a straw. The addition of the particulate filters is the defining issue of this variant. Because it uses massive turbos to push 585 hp, the heat trapped by the OPF radiates back up into the 'Hot-V'. This bakes the plastic turbo coolant lines and accelerates the failure of the active engine mounts far faster than in non-OPF models. If you track this car, deleting the OPF (where legal) is an engine-saving preventative measure.\n\nOn the positive side, you avoid the terrifyingly expensive 48V hybrid electrical architecture found in the 4-cylinder models. The M177 uses a traditional 12V starter, and the timing chain is at the front of the engine where it belongs. However, you must proactively manage mechanical brutality. The PCV (oil separator) valves clog frequently; if you ignore a whistling sound, crankcase pressure will blow out the Rear Main Seal (RMS), requiring a $3,000+ transmission drop. Change the MCT and 4MATIC+ transfer case fluid every 50,000 km. Use strictly Low-SAPS oil to protect the OPF, and preemptively install billet turbo coolant lines.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$3,500 - $6,500 / year (The GT 63 carries 'Supercar Tax', exacerbated by OPF heat soak. You MUST budget for the inevitable Hot-V Turbo Coolant Line billet upgrade ($600). If the PCV fails and blows the Rear Main Seal, expect a $3,000+ repair bill. Active Engine Mounts ($1,200) fail regularly under the V8's torque and OPF heat. Consumables are massive: a set of Michelin Pilot Sport 4S tires and Brembo 2-piece front rotors ($1,100) will vanish quickly under the heavy AWD chassis. Strict 8,000 km Low-SAPS oil changes ($160) and frequent MCT/Transfer Case services are non-negotiable.)",
