        sisterModels: [ { name: "Mercedes-AMG GT 63 S E Performance (C192)", note: "The PRO is technically the purer, lighter track weapon, abandoning the heavy plug-in hybrid battery of the 'E Performance' in favor of extreme mechanical cooling." } ], 
        tuningPotential: [
            { stage: "Stage 1 (ECU Remap)", power: "720 - 740 hp", notes: "MODERATE RISK GAINS. An OBD Stage 1 flash yields massive gains. However, tuning an OPF-equipped GT 63 PRO requires extreme caution on track. Pushing boost significantly raises Exhaust Gas Temperatures (EGTs), which will melt the restrictive particulate filters during a 20-minute track session. TCU tuning is mandatory.", zeroToHundred: "2.9s", hundredToTwoHundred: "6.9s" },
            { stage: "Stage 2 (OPF Delete & Downpipes & Tune)", power: "780 - 820 hp", notes: "Requires physical removal of the OPF filters and catless downpipes (FOR TRACK USE ONLY). This is absolutely mandatory if you intend to track a tuned PRO. It drops EGTs massively, saves the turbos from heat death, and restores the thunderous AMG V8 sound. TCU tuning is absolutely mandatory.", zeroToHundred: "2.7s", hundredToTwoHundred: "6.2s" },
            { stage: "Stage 3 (Upgraded Turbos & HPFP)", power: "900 - 950+ hp", notes: "Involves removing the factory turbos from the Hot-V and installing larger units alongside upgraded High Pressure Fuel Pumps (HPFP). The OPF must be deleted. At this level, the AMG SPEEDSHIFT MCT requires upgraded clutch packs, and the 4MATIC+ transfer case is under extreme stress.", zeroToHundred: "2.5s", hundredToTwoHundred: "5.5s" }
        ],
        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 612, nm: 850 }, stage1: { hp: 730, nm: 950 }, stage2: { hp: 800, nm: 1050 }, stage3: { hp: 920, nm: 1200 } },
        riskRating: 9,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/C192/c192.glb", model3DScale: 0.1, model3DRotation: [0, 3.14, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.13, y: 0, z: 0.35 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights.001"], taillights: ["taillights.001", "taillights.002"], drllight: ["drls.002"] }, author: ".", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 40, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 15, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The AMG GT 63 PRO (C192) is the purist's track weapon. While the E Performance relies on heavy hybrid batteries to generate power, the PRO relies on brute mechanical force and apocalyptic cooling capacity. Generating 612 hp from the unassisted V8, it utilizes aggressive active aerodynamics to suck the car to the tarmac. The defining feature is its endurance: it features dedicated radiators and electric water pumps strictly to cool the front/rear differentials and the transfer case, ensuring the 4MATIC+ system never overheats during a track day. It stops violently on standard Carbon Ceramic brakes and corners with physics-defying speed via the Active Rear Steering.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From an engineering perspective, the GT 63 PRO is spectacular, but it introduces a horrifying level of mechanical complexity strictly in the name of cooling. Because the Euro 6e OPF filters generate so much heat, and track use generates even more, AMG had to compensate. \n\nThey added three separate, dedicated electric water pumps to circulate coolant through the front differential, the rear differential, and the transfer case. If you track this car and just one of those electric pumps fails, the drivetrain will instantly overheat and throw the car into severe limp mode. The active aero components in the front fascia and underbody are brilliant, but they are fully exposed to rubber marbles and track debris, which frequently jams the actuators. \n\nFurthermore, you must proactively manage the V8's traditional brutality. The PCV (oil separator) valves clog frequently; if you ignore a whistling sound, crankcase pressure will blow out the Rear Main Seal (RMS), requiring a $3,000+ transmission drop. Change the MCT and 4MATIC+ transfer case fluid every 30,000 km if tracked. Use strictly Low-SAPS oil to protect the OPF, preemptively install billet turbo coolant lines, and perform mandatory cool-down laps so you don't oxidize the $15,000 Carbon Ceramic rotors.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$4,500 - $8,500 / year (The GT 63 PRO carries immense 'Supercar Tax', drastically exacerbated by track use and OPF heat. If you oxidize the Carbon Ceramic rotors on track, replacement is $15,000+. You MUST budget for the inevitable Hot-V Turbo Coolant Line billet upgrade ($600). If the PCV fails and blows the Rear Main Seal, expect a $3,000+ repair bill. Active Engine Mounts ($1,200) and drivetrain auxiliary water pumps fail regularly under track G-forces and heat. A set of Michelin Pilot Sport Cup 2 tires will vanish in a few track weekends. Strict 5,000 km Low-SAPS oil changes ($160) are non-negotiable.)",
