        sisterModels: [ { name: "Mercedes-AMG SL 63 S E Performance (R232)", note: "Utilizes the exact same P3 hybrid architecture, M177 V8, and F1-derived HPB battery in the SL roadster chassis." } ], 
        tuningPotential: [
            { stage: "Stage 1 (ECU Remap - ICE Only)", power: "920 - 950 hp (System)", notes: "EXTREME CAUTION. Tuning an E Performance model focuses entirely on the V8 engine; the EDU software remains locked. An OBD Stage 1 flash pushes the ICE to ~750 hp, combined with the 204 hp EV motor. Pushing boost significantly raises Exhaust Gas Temperatures (EGTs), which will melt the restrictive OPF filters. TCU tuning for the 9G MCT is absolutely mandatory.", zeroToHundred: "2.6s", hundredToTwoHundred: "6.0s" },
            { stage: "Stage 2 (OPF Delete & Downpipes & ICE Tune)", power: "980 - 1020 hp (System)", notes: "Requires physical removal of the OPF filters and catless downpipes (FOR TRACK USE ONLY). Drops EGTs massively, saves the turbos, and restores the V8 sound. The combined system torque approaches 1,600 Nm, which puts terrifying stress on the rear 2-speed EDU gearbox and 4MATIC+ clutches.", zeroToHundred: "2.5s", hundredToTwoHundred: "5.4s" },
            { stage: "Stage 3 (Upgraded Turbos & ICE Fueling)", power: "1100+ hp (System)", notes: "Upgrading the Hot-V turbos pushes the ICE alone past 900 hp. However, at this level, the vehicle is essentially an engineering experiment. The AMG HCU (Hybrid Control Unit) frequently throws torque-limit limp modes because the ICE is overwhelming the Euro 6e torque-blending algorithms.", zeroToHundred: "2.3s", hundredToTwoHundred: "4.8s" }
        ],
        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 816, nm: 1420 }, stage1: { hp: 940, nm: 1520 }, stage2: { hp: 1000, nm: 1580 }, stage3: { hp: 1100, nm: 1650 } },
        riskRating: 10,
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/GT/C192/c192.glb", model3DScale: 0.1, model3DRotation: [0, 3.14, 0], manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.13, y: 0, z: 0.35 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights.001"], taillights: ["taillights.001", "taillights.002"], drllight: ["drls.002"] }, author: ".", lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 40, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 15, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The AMG GT 63 S E Performance (C192) completely rewrites the laws of physics. Weighing nearly 2,400 kg, it shouldn't be able to move like it does. The P3 hybrid system is not for eco-driving; it is an F1-derived deployment of power. The 150 kW electric motor on the rear axle eliminates turbo lag entirely. When you initiate launch control, the 1420 Nm of system torque hits with a violence that makes your vision blur. You feel the rear 2-speed electric transmission shift seamlessly into high gear, ensuring the relentless surge of power never tapers off. It corners flatter than expected thanks to the active hydraulic roll control, but you can always feel the immense mass trying to escape.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The GT 63 S E Performance (C192) represents the absolute pinnacle of Mercedes-AMG engineering, and simultaneously, a mechanic's greatest nightmare. You are essentially maintaining two high-performance drivetrains bolted together in a 2.4-ton chassis. \n\nLet's start with the ICE. The M177 V8 still suffers from the traditional 'Hot-V' issues. The turbo coolant lines will bake and shatter; upgrade to billet immediately. The PCV system will clog and blow the Rear Main Seal (RMS). However, the real terror lies in the P3 hybrid architecture. The AMG High Performance Battery (HPB) is a 6.1 kWh marvel of F1 technology, utilizing 14 liters of specialized non-conductive dielectric fluid flowing directly over 560 individual cylindrical cells. If this internal cooling circuit springs a leak, the battery destroys itself. A replacement battery is a catastrophic five-figure expense. \n\nThe rear Electric Drive Unit (EDU) houses the motor, the e-LSD, and its own 2-speed transmission. The sheer 1420 Nm torque output literally tears the active engine mounts to shreds over time and cooks the MCT 9G wet clutch if not serviced meticulously. Even the standard Carbon Ceramic Brakes (CCB) are pushed to their absolute limits trying to stop 2.4 tons from hypercar speeds; if they oxidize, it's $15,000 for rotors alone. If this car is out of warranty, you must have an unlimited maintenance budget.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$6,000 - $15,000+ / year (This vehicle carries Hypercar-level financial risk out of warranty. If the F1-derived direct-cooled High-Voltage Battery fails, you are looking at a catastrophic replacement bill. The rear EDU 2-speed transmission is highly complex. Routine maintenance is astronomical: Active Engine Mounts ($1,200) fail constantly under 1420 Nm. A set of Michelin Pilot Sport 4S tires ($1,800) will last roughly 10,000 km under the 2.4-ton weight. If the Carbon Ceramic rotors oxidize from heat, replacement is $15,000+. You MUST proactively fix the V8's Hot-V coolant lines ($600 billet) and service the MCT/EDU fluids relentlessly.)",
