        sisterModels: [ { name: "Mercedes-Benz ML 350 BlueEFFICIENCY (W166)", note: "Shares the exact same M276 engine and 7G-Tronic Plus in a heavy SUV chassis." }, { name: "Mercedes-Benz E 350 CGI (W212 Facelift)", note: "Lighter sedan chassis running the identical direct-injected powertrain." } ],
        tuningPotential: [ { stage: "Stage 1", power: "320 hp", notes: "ECU remap optimizes the complex direct injection mapping. Minor peak horsepower gains, but smooths out mid-range torque.", zeroToHundred: "7.6s", hundredToTwoHundred: "N/A" }, { stage: "Stage 2", power: "330 hp", notes: "Requires high-flow catalytic converters. Rarely done due to the extreme sensitivity of the MED17.7.1 ECU to emission alterations.", zeroToHundred: "7.4s", hundredToTwoHundred: "N/A" }, { stage: "Stage 3", power: "450+ hp", notes: "Custom turbocharging or supercharging required. Absolutely destroys the delicate Piezo injectors and HPFP limits. Financially ruinous.", zeroToHundred: "5.9s", hundredToTwoHundred: "N/A" } ],
        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 306, nm: 370 }, stage1: { hp: 320, nm: 390 }, stage2: { hp: 330, nm: 400 }, stage3: { hp: 450, nm: 550 } },
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/R-Class/r.glb", model3DScale: 0.08, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], modelForwardDir: 0, manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.15, y: 0, z: 0.39 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights.002"], taillights: ["taillights2", "taillights.003"], drllight: ["drls1", "drls2"] }, author: "Mercedes R-class (https://skfb.ly/pANMT) by Res1n is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", riskRating: 8, lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 100, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 28, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The M276 transforms the R-Class into a surprisingly responsive highway cruiser. The direct injection engine feels vastly more energetic and urgent than the old M272, providing punchy mid-range torque that masks the vehicle's immense weight much better. Combined with the updated 7G-Tronic Plus, shifts are notably smoother and fuel economy takes a massive leap forward. However, the direct injection clatter at idle makes it sound slightly less refined and more 'diesel-like' than the whisper-quiet older V6s.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The M276 3.5L V6 represents a massive architectural shift for Mercedes-Benz, ditching the 90-degree angle of the dreaded M272 for a naturally balanced 60-degree V-angle. This inherently brilliant design completely eliminates the need for a balance shaft, eradicating the catastrophic gear failure that plagued previous R-Class models. However, while the bottom end is fundamentally bulletproof, Mercedes traded mechanical timing flaws for extreme direct injection complexity. The Bosch MED17.7.1 system utilizes delicate piezo injectors that are highly susceptible to clogging and electrical failure. When these injectors stick open, they can easily wash the cylinder walls of oil, score the block, or even cause catastrophic hydro-lock. Furthermore, the high-pressure fuel pump (HPFP) is a known weak point that can leak raw fuel directly into the crankcase, rapidly degrading engine oil and destroying bearings. Early M276 engines also suffer from a severe lack of oil pressure at the secondary timing chain tensioners on cold startups. This causes an agonizing chain rattle that, if ignored, will quickly obliterate the camshaft adjusters and chain guides. Installing the factory-mandated oil check valves behind the tensioners is an absolute, non-negotiable requirement for any prospective buyer. Beyond the engine, you are still dealing with the heavy, complex W251 chassis that devours suspension components and Airmatic struts at an alarming rate. Because the engine features direct injection, the intake valves are no longer washed by fuel, meaning severe carbon coking is guaranteed by 150,000 kilometers. You will need to budget for professional walnut blasting to restore lost power and smooth out the idle. Ultimately, the M276 is a dramatically superior engine to the M272 it replaced, offering better power and vastly improved fuel economy. However, it requires meticulous, expensive preventative maintenance to survive long-term. If you neglect the HPFP, injectors, or chain tensioners, this engine will bankrupt you faster than you can blink.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$2,000 - $4,000 / year",
        model3DHotspots: [
            { label: "Xenon Ballast & Headlight Control", x: -0.12, y: 0.0, z: 0.42, critical: true, details: "COMMON FAULT: The headlight igniter and ballast unit under the headlight assembly often corrodes due to moisture ingress in the headlight housing, causing flickering or total lamp failure." },
            { label: "Fuel Filler Tank Neck & Pump Seal", x: -0.18, y: 0.05, z: -0.15, critical: true, details: "CRITICAL VULNERABILITY: The fuel pump seal or the filler neck grommet can leak, causing a strong petrol smell inside the cabin, especially when the tank is full." },
            { label: "Rear Differential Output Seals", x: 0.10, y: -0.1, z: -0.40, critical: false, details: "MAINTENANCE POINT: The output shaft seals on the rear differential are prone to weeping oil, which can lead to low fluid levels and eventually gear whine if not monitored." },
            { label: "Door Lock Actuator & Window Regulator", x: 0.22, y: 0.05, z: 0.05, critical: false, details: "COMMON FAULT: Plastic gears in the window regulator or door lock solenoids fail, leading to slow window operation or doors failing to lock/unlock remotely." },
            { label: "Radiator End-Tank & Cooling System", x: 0, y: -0.02, z: 0.48, critical: true, details: "CRITICAL VULNERABILITY: The plastic end-tanks of the radiator become brittle over time and can crack under high pressure, leading to sudden coolant loss." },
            { label: "Steering Column Lock Module", x: 0.08, y: -0.02, z: 0.20, critical: true, details: "ELECTRONIC FAULT: The electronic steering column lock (ESL) module can fail, preventing the car from starting and requiring expensive replacement or emulator installation." }
        ]
