        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/S-Class/W140/w140.glb", model3DScale: 0.12, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], modelForwardDir: 0, manualLightPositions: {headlights: { x: 0.16, y: 0, z: 0.57 }}, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls"] }, author: "Mersedes-Benz S-class W140 (https://skfb.ly/oQTpt) by Black Snow is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", riskRating: 4, lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 15, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 13, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "Driving the S 280 is an exercise in majestic patience. The 193 horsepower and 270 Nm of torque are woefully inadequate for moving the gargantuan two-ton W140 chassis with any urgency. Pulling out into fast-moving highway traffic requires pinning the throttle to the firewall, forcing the silky-smooth inline-six to scream near its redline just to gather momentum. However, once up to speed, the engine is remarkably silent, and the car glides over the road with the isolated, vault-like solidity that made the W140 legendary.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The M104 2.8L inline-six is a masterpiece of classic Mercedes-Benz mechanical engineering, but it is tragically imprisoned in the wrong chassis. Dropping a 193-horsepower naturally aspirated six-cylinder into the gargantuan, two-ton W140 S-Class creates a vehicle that is agonizingly slow by modern standards. To merge safely onto a highway, you must pin the throttle to the floor, forcing the engine to scream near its redline just to gather momentum. However, despite the lack of thrust, the engine itself is phenomenally smooth and perfectly balanced. The cast-iron block and forged internals are virtually indestructible, capable of outlasting the life of the owner if properly lubricated. Sadly, Mercedes-Benz ruined this mechanical perfection with early 1990s environmental idealism by utilizing a biodegradable wiring harness. The engine heat bakes the eco-friendly wire insulation until it crumbles into dust, causing massive electrical shorts. These shorts will inevitably fry the ignition coils, the Electronic Throttle Actuator (ETA), and eventually the main Bosch HFM ECU, leading to catastrophic repair bills. If you are evaluating an S280, the very first thing you must do is slice open the wiring harness sheath; if you see cracked insulation, walk away or budget thousands for a replacement. The second major Achilles heel is the notoriously long aluminum cylinder head, which expands differently than the iron block. This thermal expansion inevitably causes the head gasket to fail at the right rear corner, weeping oil profusely down the block. Additionally, the front timing cover U-seal is guaranteed to leak oil onto the serpentine belt if it hasn't been resealed in the last decade. Once the wiring harness has been replaced with a modern silicone equivalent and the top end has been resealed, the M104 becomes a 500,000-kilometer tank. It is an engine that demands a high initial investment to correct factory flaws, but rewards the owner with decades of buttery-smooth, unkillable service. It is the perfect entry-level W140 for those who value majestic, slow-paced cruising over outright performance.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$1,500 - $3,500 / year",
