        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/S-Class/W140/w140.glb", model3DScale: 0.12, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], modelForwardDir: 0, manualLightPositions: {headlights: { x: 0.16, y: 0, z: 0.57 }}, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls"] }, author: "Mersedes-Benz S-class W140 (https://skfb.ly/oQTpt) by Black Snow is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", riskRating: 4, lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 15, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 13, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "Entering the W140 300 SE is like stepping into a heavily fortified bank vault. The pneumatic soft-close doors seal you inside a cabin equipped with thick double-glazed windows, instantly muting the chaotic outside world to a mere whisper. The 3.2-liter inline-six provides 231 horsepower, which is a noticeable and welcome upgrade over the sluggish 2.8L. It moves the gargantuan two-ton chassis with a sense of deliberate, dignified momentum rather than outright speed. The older 4-speed hydraulic automatic transmission slurs its shifts so smoothly that they are practically imperceptible, contributing to the feeling of gliding over the road on a cloud of isolated luxury. It is the ultimate expression of 1990s over-engineering, prioritizing supreme comfort and acoustic perfection above all else.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From a mechanical standpoint, the early M104 3.2L inline-six (in the 300 SE/SEL) deserves a low risk rating because its core cast-iron block, forged internals, and double-roller timing chain are practically immortal and will easily outlast the rest of the car if provided clean oil. This earlier LH-Jetronic version also largely avoids the catastrophic, fully biodegradable 'eco-junk' main engine wiring harness that plagues the later HFM models (though the Electronic Throttle Actuator wiring is still guaranteed to degrade). However, keeping this majestic S-Class roadworthy requires constant vigilance regarding a few notorious factory weak points. First is the EZL ignition module, which is mounted to the inner fender; you absolutely must clean and reapply thermal computer paste behind it, or it will overheat, fail, and leave you stranded with a part that is virtually impossible to find new. Secondly, keep a close eye on the right-rear corner of the cylinder head, as the long aluminum head's thermal expansion frequently causes the head gasket to weep oil externally down the block. Additionally, the front timing cover U-seal and the mechanical cooling fan clutch require proactive monitoring to prevent unexpected oil leaks or catastrophic fan explosions at high RPM. If these known vintage issues have already been addressed by a previous owner, the early 3.2L is an exceptionally reliable, vault-like cruiser that will reward you with decades of smooth, bulletproof operation.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$1,000 - $2,500 / year",
