        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/S-Class/W140/w140.glb", model3DScale: 0.12, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], modelForwardDir: 0, manualLightPositions: {headlights: { x: 0.16, y: 0, z: 0.57 }}, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls"] }, author: "Mersedes-Benz S-class W140 (https://skfb.ly/oQTpt) by Black Snow is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", riskRating: 4, lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 15, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 13, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "Entering the W140 S 320 is like stepping into a heavily fortified bank vault. The pneumatic soft-close doors seal you inside a cabin equipped with thick double-glazed windows, instantly muting the chaotic outside world to a mere whisper. The 3.2-liter inline-six provides 231 horsepower, which is a noticeable and welcome upgrade over the sluggish 2.8L. It moves the gargantuan two-ton chassis with a sense of deliberate, dignified momentum rather than outright speed. The later 5-speed 722.6 automatic transmission utilizes the torque much better than the older hydraulic units, contributing to the feeling of gliding over the road on a cloud of isolated luxury. It is the ultimate expression of 1990s over-engineering, prioritizing supreme comfort and acoustic perfection above all else.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From a mechanical standpoint, the M104 3.2L inline-six deserves a low risk rating because its core cast-iron block, forged internals, and double-roller timing chain are practically immortal and will easily outlast the rest of the car if provided clean oil. However, keeping this majestic S-Class roadworthy requires constant vigilance regarding a few notorious factory weak points that you must watch out for. First and foremost is the biodegradable eco-junk wiring harness introduced with the HFM management system; engine heat causes the original wire insulation to crumble into dust, creating short-circuits that can instantly fry the expensive Bosch HFM engine control unit or the Electronic Throttle Actuator (ETA). You must inspect the harness immediately upon purchase, and if it hasn't already been replaced with a modern silicone-insulated alternative, budget for it immediately to prevent catastrophic electrical failure. Secondly, keep a close eye on the right-rear corner of the cylinder head, as the long aluminum head's thermal expansion frequently causes the head gasket to weep oil externally down the block. Additionally, the front timing cover U-seal and the mechanical cooling fan clutch require proactive monitoring to prevent unexpected oil leaks or catastrophic fan explosions at high RPM. If these known vintage issues have already been addressed by a previous owner, the S 320 is an exceptionally reliable, vault-like cruiser that will reward you with decades of smooth, bulletproof operation.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$1,000 - $2,500 / year",
