        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/S-Class/W140/w140.glb", model3DScale: 0.12, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], modelForwardDir: 0, manualLightPositions: {headlights: { x: 0.16, y: 0, z: 0.57 }}, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls"] }, author: "Mersedes-Benz S-class W140 (https://skfb.ly/oQTpt) by Black Snow is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", riskRating: 4, lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 15, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 13, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "Entering the early W140 400 SE is like stepping into a heavily fortified bank vault, but one propelled by a deeply refined, muscular V8. The pneumatic soft-close doors seal you inside a cabin equipped with thick double-glazed windows, instantly muting the chaotic outside world to a mere whisper. The 4.2-liter M119 V8 provides 286 horsepower and 410 Nm of torque, which transforms the driving dynamic completely compared to the 6-cylinder models. It moves the gargantuan two-ton chassis with effortless, commanding authority. The early, unrestricted LH-Jetronic software provides a remarkably punchy throttle response, and the 4-speed hydraulic automatic transmission slurs its shifts so smoothly that they are practically imperceptible. It is the ultimate expression of 1990s over-engineering, blending supreme comfort with effortless V8 momentum.",
        mechanicVerdict: "From a mechanical standpoint, the M119 4.2L V8 is widely considered one of the absolute greatest, most over-engineered V8 engines Mercedes-Benz has ever produced. Its closed-deck aluminum block, forged crankshaft, and massive connecting rods are genuinely immortal and can easily withstand double the factory horsepower. Because this is the early 1991-1993 model, you benefit from the unrestricted factory software mapping, making the engine feel phenomenally responsive. However, keeping this masterpiece alive requires strict adherence to a few notorious factory flaws. The most critical issue is the upper plastic timing chain guides; these bake in the extreme V8 heat, turn brittle, and will eventually snap. If a piece falls into the timing chain path, it will derail the chain and instantly destroy the engine. You MUST proactively replace these upper guides every 100,000 kilometers. Secondly, the factory plastic camshaft oiler tubes crack and blow out, starving the cams of oil; upgrading to aftermarket aluminum tubes is a mandatory survival tactic. Thirdly, you are still dealing with the infamous 1990s biodegradable wiring harness; if the engine harness insulation is crumbling, it will short out and permanently fry the irreplaceable EZL ignition module or the LH-Jetronic ECU. Finally, you must regularly clean and reapply thermal paste behind the EZL module on the fender to prevent it from dying of heat exhaustion. If you buy a 400 SE where the wiring harness, chain guides, and oiler tubes have been addressed, you possess a legendary 500,000-kilometer V8 tank that will outlast almost anything on the modern road.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$1,500 - $3,000 / year",
