        id: "m120982-s600-me-w140", brand: "Mercedes-Benz", model: "S 600", generation: "W140 Facelift (1995-1998)", engineCode: "M 120.982", profileId: "m120982-s600-me-w140", displacement: "5,987 cc", power: "394 hp (290 kW)", torque: "570 Nm", fuelType: "Petrol", cylinders: 12, valves: 48, compression: "10.0:1", yearRange: "1995-1998",
        timingType: "Double-Roller Chain (An incredibly robust chain system. However, the upper plastic chain guides inevitably bake in the intense V12 heat. They become brittle and snap. If a piece falls into the gears, it derails the massive chain and destroys all 48 valves instantly.)", 
        injectionType: "Bosch Motronic ME 1.0 Sequential Injection. This late-stage M120 iteration finally abandons the dual LH-Jetronic ECUs in favor of a single integrated ME 1.0 network. This allows for fully digital CAN-bus communication with the ASR traction control and the new 5G-Tronic gearbox.", 
        engineLifespan: "400,000 - 600,000+ km (The closed-deck Alusil block is essentially bulletproof. The engine's death usually comes from severe neglect of the plastic timing guides or catastrophic failure of the cooling system causing the massive block to warp.)", 
        turboType: "Naturally Aspirated (Relies on massive displacement and quad-cam, 48-valve flow efficiency to generate an unstoppable, turbine-smooth wave of 570 Nm of torque.)", 
        ecuType: "Bosch Motronic ME 1.0 (A massive technological leap that permanently solved the terrifying EZL heat-death and finally phased out the catastrophic biodegradable wiring harnesses of the earlier years.)", 
        euroStandard: "Euro 2", 
        maxPowerStockInternals: "800+ hp (The forged crankshaft and massive connecting rods are vastly overbuilt. Pagani utilized this exact bottom-end architecture for the Zonda because it handles staggering amounts of horsepower without flinching.)",
