        tuningGraphData: { stock: { hp: 204, nm: 270 }, stage1: { hp: 215, nm: 285 }, stage2: { hp: 225, nm: 300 }, stage3: { hp: 300, nm: 420 } },
        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/S-Class/W220/w220.glb", model3DScale: 0.15, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], modelForwardDir: 0, manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.13, y: -0.02, z: 0.55 } },  customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights1", "headlights2"], taillights: ["chrome.012"], drllight: ["drls1", "drls2"] }, author: "Mercedes-Benz S-Class 1979-2005 (https://skfb.ly/pFxMS) by Merc_TV is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", riskRating: 4, lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 20, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 18, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The S 280 in the W220 chassis is a prime example of being severely under-engined. The 204 horsepower M112 V6 struggles significantly to motivate the heavy 2-ton luxury barge, requiring frequent, harsh downshifts from the 722.6 transmission just to maintain highway speeds on slight inclines. The V6 lacks the effortless, wafting low-end torque of the V8 models (like the S430 or S500), making city driving feel surprisingly strained and unrefined. However, once you patiently reach a steady cruising speed, the W220's Airmatic suspension and double-glazed windows provide an unparalleled, cloud-like ride. It is whisper-quiet and isolating—provided the air suspension hasn't collapsed.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The M112 2.8L V6 is, ironically, the most reliable component in the entire W220 S-Class. The engine block is practically bulletproof, utilizing a robust timing chain and forged internals that will easily eclipse 400,000 kilometers with basic maintenance. However, it does have a few infamous quirks, such as the harmonic balancer that delaminates and literally saws a hole into the timing cover if ignored. The Crankshaft Position Sensor (CPS) is also guaranteed to fail from heat soak, leaving you stranded in traffic with a car that refuses to start until it cools down. Servicing the ignition is unnecessarily expensive because the twin-spark cylinder heads require 12 spark plugs for a 6-cylinder engine. Valve cover gaskets leak profusely due to brittle PCV hoses clogging the breather chambers, forcing oil down onto the exhaust manifolds. But the true horror of the S 280 lies not in the engine, but in the W220 chassis wrapped around it. The Airmatic suspension is a ticking financial time bomb; the struts crack, the valve blocks leak, and the compressor relay sticks closed, burning the pump to a crisp. If the cowl drains clog with leaves, rainwater floods the passenger footwell and instantly destroys the SAM modules and the Transmission Control Unit, totaling the car electronically. Early models (1998-2002) also suffer from catastrophic rust issues on the doors and wheel arches. The 722.6 transmission is strong but suffers from a leaking pilot bushing that allows ATF to wick all the way up the wiring harness into the computer. Ultimately, while the M112 engine won't kill you, the electronic and pneumatic nightmares of the W220 chassis will bankrupt you without hesitation.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$2,000 - $4,500 / year",
