        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/S-Class/W220/w220.glb", model3DScale: 0.15, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], modelForwardDir: 0, manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.13, y: -0.02, z: 0.55 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights1", "headlights2"], taillights: ["chrome.012"], drllight: ["drls1", "drls2"] }, author: "Mercedes-Benz S-Class 1979-2005 (https://skfb.ly/pFxMS) by Merc_TV is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", riskRating: 7, lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 20, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 18, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The early S 400 CDI offers a commanding, deeply satisfying driving experience when functioning correctly. The twin-turbo V8 diesel produces a massive 560 Nm of torque at very low RPMs, allowing the heavy S-Class to surge forward with effortless, locomotive-like thrust. It lacks the higher-revving, frantic nature of the petrol V8s, but compensates with a deep, muffled diesel burble and excellent long-distance highway cruising capability. The Airmatic suspension maintains the classic W220 cloud-like ride, perfectly isolating you from the road.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The early OM628 4.0L V8 diesel is an engine that looks fantastic on paper but is notoriously difficult to maintain in reality. Mercedes packed two turbochargers, dual ECUs, and a highly complex EGR system into a very tight engine bay. The biggest Achilles heel of the OM628 is the timing chain; the immense torque and engine architecture cause the chain to stretch prematurely. If ignored, the chain will skip, destroying the engine. Replacing it is a highly labor-intensive, expensive job. The dual EGR valves also choke the intake manifolds with thick soot, requiring frequent scraping and cleaning. Furthermore, accessing the rear fuel injectors against the firewall to fix the common 'Black Death' seal leaks is incredibly cramped. Because this is the initial 2000-2003 version, it is housed in the pre-facelift W220 chassis, meaning you also have to fight horrific rust issues on the doors and wheel arches, alongside the usual Airmatic and SAM module flooding vulnerabilities. It is a torque monster, but maintenance costs can quickly exceed the value of the rusty car.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$2,500 - $5,000 / year",
