        id: "m272946-s300-w221", brand: "Mercedes-Benz", model: "S 300", generation: "W221 Facelift (2005-2013)", engineCode: "M 272.946", profileId: "m272946-s300-w221", displacement: "2,996 cc", power: "231 hp (170 kW)", torque: "300 Nm", fuelType: "Petrol", cylinders: 6, valves: 24, compression: "11.3:1", yearRange: "2009-2013",
        timingType: "Double-Roller Chain (Highly Robust. Because the S 300 was introduced with the 2009 facelift, it fundamentally benefits from Mercedes replacing the defective sintered metal balance shaft sprocket at the factory. The timing system in these late-model M272 engines is extremely durable and rarely requires major service if oil changes are maintained.)", 
        injectionType: "Sequential Multi-Point Port Injection. Bosch Motronic ME 9.7. Features updated Euro 5 emissions mapping, an intelligent alternator management system to reduce parasitic drag, and the highly complex dual-stage variable intake manifold with internal tumble flaps.", 
        engineLifespan: "300,000 - 500,000+ km (By surviving the balance shaft crisis of the pre-facelift era, the 3.0L M272 in the S 300 becomes an incredibly reliable, albeit underpowered, workhorse. It will easily outlast the complex air suspension and interior electronics of the W221 chassis.)", 
        turboType: "Naturally Aspirated (Relies heavily on the variable intake manifold and the tightly packed ratios of the 7G-Tronic transmission to provide adequate acceleration. It is sufficient for relaxed city driving, but severely lacks the effortless highway passing power associated with the S-Class badge.)", 
        ecuType: "Bosch Motronic ME 9.7 (A sophisticated computer that remains highly vulnerable to engine oil intrusion. Leaking camshaft adjuster magnets can allow oil to wick up the wiring harness via capillary action directly into the ECU, permanently frying the motherboard.)", 
        euroStandard: "Euro 5", 
        maxPowerStockInternals: "350+ hp (The aluminum block with Silitec liners is very strong, but aftermarket tuning is highly discouraged. Modest gains are not worth the stress, and owners should appreciate the vehicle for its serene cruising nature rather than raw speed.)",
