        drivingExperience: "The S 300 serves as the entry-level gateway to the facelifted W221 generation. It offers all the visual prestige, sweeping exterior lines, and incredible interior technology of the flagship models, but it is mated to a relatively small 3.0L V6. Generating 231 horsepower and 300 Nm of torque, the M272 engine has to work noticeably hard to motivate the heavy 2-ton luxury barge. You will hear the V6 revving much higher in city traffic compared to the whisper-quiet V8 models. Fortunately, the 7G-Tronic transmission has enough closely spaced gear ratios to keep the engine in its narrow powerband, masking the lack of low-end grunt fairly well. Once up to highway speeds, the W221's phenomenal acoustic insulation and standard Airmatic suspension provide a serene, cloud-like ride.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The M272 3.0L V6 inside the facelifted W221 S 300 is a story of redemption. Because the S 300 badge was introduced in 2009 alongside the mid-cycle refresh, it completely avoids the catastrophic 'balance shaft failure' that destroyed so many early M272 engines. Mercedes finally utilized hardened steel for the balance shaft sprocket at the factory. With the timing gear issue solved, the S 300 is fundamentally a very durable, albeit underpowered, luxury cruiser. However, it still suffers from infuriating accessory flaws. The plastic tumble flaps inside the complex intake manifold become brittle and break off, forcing you to buy a $1,000 manifold or risk internal engine damage if the flaps get sucked into a cylinder. Furthermore, the four camshaft adjuster magnets leak oil through their electrical connectors; this oil travels via capillary action up the wiring harness and directly into the Bosch ME9.7 ECU, completely destroying the computer. You must install 'pigtail' isolation harnesses immediately to save the ECU. While the early 7G-Tronic transmissions suffered heavily, the facelift models are generally smoother, though they still require strict 60k km fluid changes. You still have to contend with the inevitable Airmatic suspension leaks, but compared to an early W221, the facelifted S 300 is a drastically safer financial proposition.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$1,500 - $3,500 / year",
