        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/S-Class/W221/w221.glb", model3DScale: 0.18, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], modelForwardDir: 0, manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.15, y: -0.02, z: 0.52 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls1", "drls2"] }, author: "Mersedes-Benz S-class W221 (https://skfb.ly/oTZ8N) by Black Snow is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", riskRating: 6, lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 70, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 12, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The S 350 is the standard global workhorse of the W221 generation. Generating 272 horsepower and 350 Nm of torque, the M272 engine handles the heavy 2-ton luxury barge adequately, though it requires frequent downshifts from the 7G-Tronic transmission to maintain momentum on steep inclines or during highway overtakes. The V6 fundamentally lacks the deep, effortless thrust of the V8 models, but the 7-speed transmission masks this relatively well by keeping the engine in its powerband. Once up to highway speeds, the W221's phenomenal acoustic insulation and standard Airmatic suspension provide a serene, cloud-like ride. The interior technology and build quality represent a massive leap forward from the previous generation.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The M272 3.5L V6 inside the pre-facelift W221 is a spectacular luxury vehicle utterly compromised by a few fatal engineering flaws. Between 2005 and late 2008, Mercedes utilized an improperly hardened sintered metal for the engine's central balance shaft sprocket. The timing chain literally chews the teeth off this gear, throwing timing codes (P0016/P0017) and ultimately requiring the entire engine to be removed and split open to fix it—an incredibly expensive repair. If you are buying an early S 350, you MUST verify that the engine serial number falls outside the affected range, or that the balance shaft has already been replaced with documented proof. Beyond the balance shaft, the plastic tumble flaps inside the complex intake manifold become brittle and break off, forcing you to buy a $1,000 manifold or risk internal engine damage. Furthermore, the four camshaft adjuster magnets leak oil through their electrical connectors; this oil travels via capillary action up the wiring harness and directly into the Bosch ME9.7 ECU, completely destroying the computer. You must install 'pigtail' isolation harnesses immediately to save the ECU. The early 722.9 7G-Tronic transmissions also suffer from failing Siemens conductor plates, trapping the car in limp mode. The Airmatic suspension will eventually leak, dropping the car to the ground. If you find an S 350 where the balance shaft, intake manifold, and conductor plate have already been fixed, it is a lovely, comfortable car. If not, it is a ticking financial time bomb.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$2,500 - $5,500 / year",
