        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/S-Class/W221/w221.glb", model3DScale: 0.18, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], modelForwardDir: 0, manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.15, y: -0.02, z: 0.52 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls1", "drls2"] }, author: "Mersedes-Benz S-class W221 (https://skfb.ly/oTZ8N) by Black Snow is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", riskRating: 5, lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 70, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 12, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The S 350 4MATIC offers a highly secure, composed, and isolating driving experience. Generating 272 horsepower and 350 Nm of torque, the M272 engine has adequate power, but the addition of the 4MATIC all-wheel-drive system adds significant weight and parasitic drag, making the V6 feel somewhat sluggish off the line compared to the RWD model. However, where it loses in raw acceleration, it gains in absolute all-weather confidence. The 4ETS system perfectly distributes torque in rain or snow, making this an unstoppable winter cruiser. The 7G-Tronic transmission shifts smoothly, and the standard Airmatic suspension completely erases road imperfections, providing a cloud-like ride.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The late-model M272 3.5L V6 inside the S 350 4MATIC is generally a very safe and reliable powertrain, primarily because it completely avoids the catastrophic 'balance shaft failure' that plagued engines built before late 2007. Mercedes finally utilized hardened steel for the balance shaft sprocket at the factory. However, this engine still suffers from highly annoying accessory flaws. The plastic tumble flaps inside the complex intake manifold become brittle and break off, forcing you to buy a $1,000 manifold or risk internal engine damage if the flaps get sucked into a cylinder. Furthermore, the four camshaft adjuster magnets leak oil through their electrical connectors; this oil travels via capillary action up the wiring harness and directly into the Bosch ME9.7 ECU, completely destroying the computer. You must install 'pigtail' isolation harnesses immediately to save the ECU. Because it is a 4MATIC model, you must also maintain the transfer case fluid; if ignored, the internal bearings will whine and the chain will stretch. The Airmatic suspension will eventually leak, dropping the car to the ground. Overall, if you proactively manage the intake manifold, cam magnets, and transfer case fluid, this is a highly dependable, all-weather luxury fortress.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$1,800 - $3,500 / year",
