        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/S-Class/W221/w221.glb", model3DScale: 0.18, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], modelForwardDir: 0, manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.15, y: -0.02, z: 0.52 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls1", "drls2"] }, author: "Mersedes-Benz S-class W221 (https://skfb.ly/oTZ8N) by Black Snow is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", riskRating: 5, lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 70, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 12, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "The S 350 BlueEFFICIENCY is a dramatic leap forward from the older M272-equipped models. Generating 306 horsepower and 370 Nm of torque, the M276 engine feels significantly crisper, more responsive, and surprisingly eager to rev. The introduction of Direct Injection and the upgraded 7G-Tronic Plus transmission entirely eliminates the sluggish, strained feeling of the earlier entry-level S-Class models. It cruises with near-silent authority on the highway while delivering remarkably impressive fuel economy, aided by the ECO start-stop system in city traffic. The Airmatic suspension maintains the legendary, cloud-like ride, making it a phenomenally refined daily driver.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The M276 3.5L V6 Direct Injection engine is a monumental architectural upgrade over the M272, returning to a naturally balanced 60-degree V-angle and permanently eliminating the disastrous balance shaft failure. However, this early M276 introduces its own highly specific headaches that an owner MUST address. The most famous is the cold-start timing chain rattle; Mercedes forgot to install check valves behind the hydraulic chain tensioners. The oil drains out overnight, causing the chain to violently slap the guides on cold starts. You must install the check valves and updated tensioners immediately, or the chain will stretch and destroy the engine. Furthermore, because it is a Direct Injection engine, the intake valves are no longer washed by fuel. They will cake with thick, black carbon soot over time, eventually causing severe misfires and rough idling. A physical 'walnut blasting' of the intake ports is required every 100,000 km to restore performance. The High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) is also a known failure point, throwing the car into limp mode when it dies. Beyond the engine, you still have to contend with the inevitable W221 Airmatic suspension leaks and ensure the cowl drains are clear to prevent the SAM modules from flooding. If the timing chain check valves have been installed and the intake valves are clean, this is a spectacular, modern, and highly reliable engine.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$2,000 - $4,000 / year",
