        model3DPath: "/images/brands/mercedes/S-Class/W221/w221.glb", model3DScale: 0.18, model3DRotation: [0, 0, 0], modelForwardDir: 0, manualLightPositions: { headlights: { x: 0.15, y: -0.02, z: 0.52 } }, customLightNames: { headlights: ["headlights"], taillights: ["taillights"], drllight: ["drls1", "drls2"] }, author: "Mersedes-Benz S-class W221 (https://skfb.ly/oTZ8N) by Black Snow is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).", riskRating: 10, lightSettings: { headlightAngle: 0.25, headlightIntensity: 100, headlightOpacity: 0.5, headlightRadiusTop: 0.02, headlightTargetDistance: 4.5, headlightPenumbra: 0.1, meshGlowHeadlight: 70, meshGlowTaillight: 4, meshGlowDrl: 12, useBloom: true }, hotspotSettings: { distanceFactor: 0.6 },
        drivingExperience: "Driving the S 400 Hybrid is a remarkably smooth, futuristic experience that belies the car's age. It was the first production car to use a lithium-ion battery, and the integration is astonishingly refined. The 20-horsepower electric motor is sandwiched inside the transmission, providing 160 Nm of instant, silent torque that perfectly masks the weak low-end of the Atkinson-cycle V6. The transitions between pure electric gliding, regenerative braking, and internal combustion power are almost imperceptible. In stop-and-go city traffic, it glides forward effortlessly in eerie silence, while the Airmatic suspension completely isolates you from the chaos outside.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The S 400 Hybrid is an engineering marvel that should be admired from a distance, but NEVER purchased secondhand. It is an unmitigated financial time bomb. While the internal combustion M272 V6 is the later, reliable variant (freed from the balance shaft flaw), Mercedes made a fatal packaging error with the hybrid components. They placed the highly sensitive, incredibly expensive high-voltage lithium-ion battery directly inside the scorching hot engine bay, right against the firewall. Over time, the extreme thermal cycling completely degrades the battery chemistry. Furthermore, the Power Electronics Module (the DC/DC inverter/converter) sits directly on top of the engine block. It literally cooks itself to death. If either the battery or the inverter throws a fault, the car will entirely immobilize itself—you cannot even start the petrol engine. Replacing the battery at the dealer costs over $10,000, frequently exceeding the entire value of the car. The high-voltage AC compressor is also known to fail; if a mechanic uses standard PAG oil instead of the specific POE insulating oil, the high-voltage system will ground out through the AC lines and brick the car. Add in the inevitable Airmatic suspension leaks, the plastic intake manifold flaps breaking, and leaking camshaft magnets flooding the ECU, and you have a vehicle that will financially ruin you without warning. Avoid at all costs.",
        repairCostEstimate: "$4,000 - $10,000+ / year",
