        id: "m279980-maybach-s680-w223", brand: "Mercedes-Benz", model: "Maybach S 680 4MATIC", generation: "W223 (2020-present)", engineCode: "M 279.980", profileId: "m279980-maybach-s680-w223", displacement: "5,980 cc", power: "612 hp (450 kW)", torque: "900 Nm", fuelType: "Petrol", cylinders: 12, valves: 36, compression: "9.0:1", yearRange: "2021-present",
        timingType: "Double-Roller Chain (A massively overbuilt mechanical timing system that is bathed in oil. Chain stretch is virtually unheard of. However, if any timing component or gasket on the front cover ever requires service, the incredibly tight packaging of the 6.0L block combined with the new 4MATIC front differential makes it a mandatory engine-out nightmare.)", 
        injectionType: "Sequential Multi-Point Port Injection. Bosch Motronic ME17.7.8. In an era of Direct Injection, the M279 brilliantly retains port injection. This means fuel constantly washes the intake valves, permanently preventing the catastrophic carbon soot buildup that plagues modern V8 and Inline-6 engines.", 
        engineLifespan: "250,000 - 500,000+ km (The mechanical bottom end of the M279 V12 is practically immortal, featuring a forged steel crankshaft and massive forged connecting rods. It entirely avoids the cylinder wall scoring seen in the V8s. The engine's lifespan is not dictated by internal failure, but entirely by the owner's willingness to endure the agonizing labor costs associated with the W223's V12 + 4MATIC packaging.)", 
        turboType: "Twin Turbochargers (Liquid-to-Air Intercooled). Specifically calibrated for Maybach luxury. The engine generates so much raw torque that the ECU must artificially and smoothly cut boost to cap the output at 900 Nm. The software is intentionally programmed with 'Chauffeur' mode, deliberately slowing throttle response to ensure the rear passengers are never violently jerked during acceleration.", 
