        drivingExperience: "The 2023+ S 450 d equipped with the OM 656 M Mild Hybrid engine is an absolute triumph of diesel engineering. Generating 367 mechanical horsepower plus an additional 22 hp from the electric motor, it produces a staggering combined 750 Nm of torque. It effortlessly shoves the heavy S-Class chassis forward with the brutal authority of a V8. By integrating the 48-volt EQ Boost Integrated Starter Generator (ISG), Mercedes has completely eliminated the traditional clunky diesel start-stop cycle; the engine wakes up instantly and silently. The ISG provides immediate electric torque from a dead stop, completely masking any mechanical turbo lag before the VGT turbocharger fully spools. The result is a surging, relentless wave of torque that feels inexhaustible. The 9G-Tronic transmission shifts invisibly, and at highway speeds, the engine is utterly imperceptible, returning fuel economy figures that defy physics for a vehicle capable of this level of acceleration.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The OM 656 M (Mild Hybrid) in the S 450 d represents a double-edged sword for technicians. On one hand, the internal combustion core remains the brilliant, Nanoslide-coated straight-six that is essentially bulletproof. On the other hand, Mercedes engineers bolted the incredibly complex and historically fragile 48V ISG mild-hybrid architecture to their most powerful diesel to meet strict Euro 6e emissions. By deleting the traditional serpentine belt, the water pump and AC compressor are now 48V electrically driven. If the 48V Lithium-Ion battery in the trunk degrades or the ISG fails—and it does fail—the car is completely immobilized and cannot even crank. Replacing the ISG requires dropping the entire 9G-Tronic transmission, resulting in a staggering labor bill. Furthermore, the OM 656 M is choked by incredibly strict Euro 6e emissions systems. The dual-loop EGR, DPF, and dual SCR catalytic converters with AdBlue injection are highly sensitive to driving habits. Upstream and downstream NOx sensors face intense thermal stress and frequently trigger the dreaded no-start countdown if they detect a fault. In this high-output S 450 d variant, the 750 Nm of torque pushes the 9G-Tronic transmission and 4MATIC transfer case to their absolute physical limits, necessitating strictly enforced fluid changes. Ultimately, the mild-hybrid S 450 d is a masterpiece of refinement, power, and efficiency under a factory warranty, but out of warranty, you are entirely at the mercy of its hyper-complex 48V electronics and fragile emissions sensors.",
